; The negative product at a boundary, on the native Go target.
;
; `values` is reader sugar for `(fn (#k) (#k a b))` — a function that answers
; whichever observation you give it. That is the NEGATIVE product of linear
; logic's `&`, and it is why the shape measured 1.01x with zero allocations
; (product-2026-08-19): a caller in the same reduction takes it apart before
; anything is built.
;
; This file is the OTHER case — the one that survives reduction, because the
; export is a boundary and the caller is hand-written Go. Three of our four
; targets have a native form for it; Go returns two values in registers, so
; there is still nothing to build.
;
; Two forms carried, which is the rule for adding to the gauntlet:
;
;   divmod      returns both results
;   divmod-sum  consumes them in the same reduction, so the product vanishes
;
; Scheme's `values` and Common Lisp's are deliberately not data structures, for
; exactly this reason: an implementation should return several results in
; registers rather than box them so the caller can unbox them.

(use go)

(export divmod divmod-sum)

(sig divmod     ((a int) (b int)) (int int) (where (go.!= b 0)))
; THE SUM NEEDS A BOUND ON `a`, and 2^52-1 is derived rather than picked.
; `q` is a/b and `r` is a%b, so |q| <= |a| and |r| <= |a| whatever b is --
; therefore |q + r| <= 2|a|, and the sum stays inside ADR 0012's window exactly
; when |a| <= (2^53-1)/2. Bounding `b` as well buys nothing: the analysis gets
; both the quotient and the remainder from `a` alone.
;
; Without it this is REFUSED, which is ADR 0019 working -- outside the window Go
; and the JVM wrap and JavaScript loses precision, so the program has to say
; what it means.
(sig divmod-sum ((a (int -4503599627370495 4503599627370495)) (b int)) int
  (where (go.!= b 0)))

(def dm (fn (a b) (values (go./ a b) (go.% a b))))

(def divmod     (fn (a b) (dm a b)))
(def divmod-sum (fn (a b) ((dm a b) (fn (q r) (go.+ q r)))))
